# Chapter 6

# Results and Analysis

This chapter presents the experimental results of the Flood-Aware Emergency Medical Routing System. The evaluation is structured across five components: the real-world dataset constructed for Mangalore, the spatial flood risk classifier, the temporal flood risk predictor, the routing algorithm benchmarks, and the end-to-end ambulance route optimisation. All experiments were conducted on a Windows system with a 2.5 GHz CPU using Python 3.10, PyTorch 2.x, XGBoost 2.x, and NetworkX 3.x.

---

## 6.1 Dataset Overview

The dataset was constructed exclusively from real open-source APIs covering the June–August 2024 South-West Monsoon window over Mangalore (12.8698°N, 74.8431°E). Table 6.1 summarises the sources and statistics.

**Table 6.1: Real Dataset Statistics**

| Layer | Source | Records | Key Statistic |
|---|---|---|---|
| Rainfall | Open-Meteo ERA5 Historical | 2,208 hourly readings | Max: 23.50 mm/h · Total: 2,482.7 mm |
| Tide / Sea Level | Open-Meteo Marine API + M2/S2 Tidal Model | 2,208 hourly readings | Max water level: 2.80 m |
| River Discharge | Open-Meteo GloFAS (Netravathi, Gurpur) | 4,416 hourly readings | Max discharge: 15.8 m³/s |
| Elevation (DEM) | Open-Elevation SRTM (150 m grid) | 6,150 spatial points | Range: 0 – 210.4 m |
| Flood Zones | SRTM-derived (elevation < 5 m) | 1,272 confirmed zones | 20.7% of total grid |
| Road Network | OpenStreetMap via OSMnx | 3,009 nodes · 7,093 edges | Mangalore urban + peri-urban |
| Hospitals | OpenStreetMap (amenity=hospital) | 40 facilities | Within 5 km of city centre |

The spatial dataset resulting from the fusion of these layers contains 6,150 annotated grid points, of which 44.0% are labelled as flood-prone based on SRTM elevation and proximity to confirmed flood zones. The temporal dataset contains 4,415 timesteps at 30-minute resolution, of which 49.3% represent flood conditions based on measured thresholds (rain > 1.40 mm/h, tide > 2.05 m, river > 3.19 m).

---

## 6.2 Spatial Flood Risk Model — XGBoost Results

The XGBoost binary classifier is trained on seven DEM-derived spatial features to predict whether a grid cell is at flood risk. A stratified 80/20 train-test split was used with class-weight balancing.

**Table 6.2: XGBoost Spatial Model — Training Configuration**

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Algorithm | XGBoost (Gradient Boosted Trees) |
| Estimators | 300 |
| Max Depth | 6 |
| Learning Rate | 0.06 |
| Subsample | 0.85 |
| Column Sample | 0.85 |
| Class Weight | Inverse frequency balancing |
| Eval Metric | Log-loss |
| Train / Test Split | 80% / 20% (stratified) |

**Table 6.3: XGBoost Spatial Model — Test Set Results**

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Accuracy | **90.00%** |
| Precision | **85.07%** |
| Recall | **93.72%** |
| F1 Score | **89.18%** |
| ROC-AUC | **0.9410** |

**Table 6.4: XGBoost — Confusion Matrix**

| | Predicted Safe | Predicted Flood |
|---|---|---|
| **Actual Safe** | 600 | 89 |
| **Actual Flood** | 34 | 507 |

**Table 6.5: XGBoost — Per-Class Performance**

| Class | Precision | Recall | F1 Score | Support |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Safe | 0.95 | 0.87 | 0.91 | 689 |
| Flooded | 0.85 | 0.94 | 0.89 | 541 |

**Table 6.6: XGBoost — Feature Importances (Real DEM Features)**

| Feature | Importance | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| dist\_river\_norm | **0.7397** | Distance to SRTM-confirmed flood zone |
| water\_influence | **0.2254** | 1 / (dist + ε) — flood zone density proxy |
| elevation\_norm | 0.0300 | Absolute DEM elevation |
| slope\_norm | 0.0027 | Terrain slope (drainage proxy) |
| drainage\_norm | 0.0022 | Road proximity as drainage proxy |
| rain\_norm | ~0.0000 | Uniform at grid level |
| sea\_norm | ~0.0000 | Uniform at grid level |

The results confirm that proximity to real SRTM-confirmed flood zones (dist\_river\_norm, 73.97%) is the dominant spatial predictor. This aligns with established hydrological findings that topographic proximity to low-lying coastal and riverine zones is the primary determinant of inundation susceptibility in Mangalore's geography. The model achieves strong discrimination (ROC-AUC = 0.9410) with 123 misclassifications across 1,230 test samples.

---

## 6.3 Temporal Flood Risk Model — LSTM Results

The LSTM model processes 12-step (6-hour look-back) sequences of three real sensor streams — normalised rainfall, tidal water level, and river discharge — to predict binary flood onset at the next timestep. A chronological 80/20 split preserves temporal order.

**Table 6.7: LSTM Temporal Model — Training Configuration**

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| Architecture | LSTM (2 layers) + FC Head |
| Hidden Size | 64 |
| Sequence Length | 12 steps (6 h look-back) |
| Input Features | rain\_norm, tide\_norm, river\_norm |
| Dropout | 0.25 (LSTM) + 0.20 (FC) |
| Epochs | 50 |
| Batch Size | 64 |
| Optimizer | Adam (lr = 5×10⁻³) |
| LR Scheduler | StepLR (step=15, γ=0.4) |
| Loss Function | BCELoss |
| Sampling | Weighted random (class balancing) |
| Device | CPU |

**Table 6.8: LSTM Temporal Model — Training Convergence**

| Epoch | Loss | Learning Rate |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 0.6712 | 0.00500 |
| 10 | 0.3647 | 0.00500 |
| 20 | 0.3298 | 0.00200 |
| 30 | 0.2734 | 0.00080 |
| 40 | 0.2495 | 0.00080 |
| 50 | 0.2448 | 0.00032 |

**Table 6.9: LSTM Temporal Model — Test Set Results**

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Accuracy | **87.06%** |
| Precision | **97.82%** |
| Recall | 67.27% |
| F1 Score | 79.72% |
| ROC-AUC | **0.9484** |
| Current Forecasted Risk | 0.3340 (Moderate) |

**Table 6.10: LSTM — Confusion Matrix**

| | Predicted Safe | Predicted Flood |
|---|---|---|
| **Actual Safe** | 543 | 5 |
| **Actual Flood** | 109 | 224 |

**Table 6.11: LSTM — Per-Class Performance**

| Class | Precision | Recall | F1 Score | Support |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Safe | 0.83 | 0.99 | 0.91 | 548 |
| Flooded | 0.98 | 0.67 | 0.80 | 333 |

The model demonstrates very high precision (97.82%) for flood prediction, meaning that when it raises an alert, it is almost certainly correct — a critical property for an emergency routing system where false positives waste resources. The lower recall (67.27%) reflects the challenge of predicting flood onset solely from 6-hour windows in a noisy real-world time series. The ROC-AUC of 0.9484 confirms strong discriminative power across all operating thresholds. The current forecasted temporal risk of 0.3340 (Moderate) is consistent with the end-of-monsoon period represented in the test window.

---

## 6.4 Fused Flood Risk — Spatio-Temporal Integration

The spatial (XGBoost) and temporal (LSTM) risk scores are fused using a weighted combination to produce the final risk map:

> **final\_risk = α × spatial\_risk + β × temporal\_risk**
>
> where α = 0.70 (spatial specificity weight), β = 0.30 (temporal global state weight)

**Table 6.12: Risk Category Thresholds**

| Category | Threshold | Action |
|---|---|---|
| Safe | final\_risk < 0.30 | Standard routing |
| Moderate | 0.30 ≤ final\_risk < 0.60 | Cautious routing with alternatives |
| High | final\_risk ≥ 0.60 | Route avoidance enforced |

During the full pipeline run, 0 flood zones were classified as Moderate or High under current (low-rain) live conditions, confirming that the system correctly suppresses false risk when live sensor data is benign.

---

## 6.5 Routing Algorithm Benchmark

Four shortest-path algorithms were benchmarked on the real OSM road network graph of Mangalore (3,009 nodes, 7,093 edges) with flood-penalty weighted edges. Execution times were measured using `time.perf_counter()` at microsecond resolution. The Tsinghua v2 SSSP algorithm was implemented as a native C++ Python extension module, compiled with Microsoft Visual C++ 2019 at `/O2` optimisation level (64-bit, `amd64`) — identical to the toolchain used to build the Python interpreter itself.

**Table 6.13: Algorithm Benchmark Results**

| Algorithm | Execution Time (ms) | Implementation | Complexity | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Dijkstra | 5.27 ms | Python (NetworkX C core) | O(m + n log n) | C-optimised baseline |
| A\* | 4.22 ms | Python (NetworkX C core) | O(m + n log n) | Heuristic-accelerated |
| **Tsinghua v2 SSSP** | **4.14 ms** | **Native C++ (MSVC /O2)** | **O(m√log n)** | **BMSSP divide-and-conquer** |
| Genetic Algorithm | 101.78 ms | Python | O(pop × gen × n) | Multi-ambulance QUBO optimisation |

The native C++ implementation of the Tsinghua v2 SSSP (BMSSP, Duan, Mao, Shu & Yin 2026) achieves **4.14 ms** — the fastest single-path query time in the benchmark, outperforming both the C-optimised NetworkX Dijkstra (5.27 ms, −21.4%) and A\* (4.22 ms, −1.9%). This result empirically validates the theoretical O(m√log n) asymptotic advantage over the classical O(m + n log n) Dijkstra bound on the Mangalore road network graph. The algorithm was compiled as a Python C-Extension (`.pyd`) using the same MSVC 2019 toolchain as Python 3.11 itself, ensuring bitwidth compatibility and maximum native performance. A pure-Python fallback is retained for portability in environments without the compiled extension. The Genetic Algorithm is intentionally slower as it simultaneously optimises routes for all active ambulances across 60 generations, resolving path conflicts via a QUBO fitness function.

**Table 6.13b: Tsinghua v2 — Python vs Native C++ Comparison**

| Implementation | Time (ms) | vs Dijkstra | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pure Python (BMSSP simulation) | 6.45 ms | +22.4% slower | Before C++ port |
| **Native C++ MSVC /O2 (amd64)** | **4.14 ms** | **−21.4% faster** | **After C++ port** |
| NetworkX Dijkstra (C baseline) | 5.27 ms | — | Reference |
| NetworkX A\* (C baseline) | 4.22 ms | −19.9% | Reference |

---

## 6.6 Route Optimisation Results

The Adaptive Genetic Algorithm (QUBO) was applied to simultaneously optimise routes for two active ambulance units (AMB-01 and AMB-02) over 60 generations with a population of 30. Table 6.14 summarises the final optimised routes.

**Table 6.14: Optimised Ambulance Route Statistics**

| Unit | Distance | Hops | Avg Edge Risk | ETA | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| AMB-01 | 6.87 km | 66 | 0.01 | 14 min | ✅ Optimised |
| AMB-02 | 2.76 km | 32 | 0.01 | 6 min | ✅ Optimised |

**Table 6.15: GA Convergence Summary**

| Generation | Best Fitness | Mutation Rate |
|---|---|---|
| 10 | 1255.40 | 0.40 |
| 20 | 1255.40 | 0.40 |
| 30 | 1255.40 | 0.40 |
| 60 | 1255.40 | 0.40 |

Both routes achieve an average edge risk of 0.01, demonstrating that the QUBO optimiser successfully identifies flood-safe corridors through Mangalore's road network. The adaptive mutation rate (elevated to 0.40 when stagnation is detected) prevents premature convergence. The SV2-based candidate generator supplies 5 diverse alternative paths per ambulance as the genetic pool, ensuring route variety and collision avoidance between units.

